{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Y9G71cquqL8V8LxzRsHugx","type":"tool_result","content":"85:## Notes on the rows that are undefined behaviour in C\n\n## Notes on the rows that are undefined behaviour in C\n\nRows **E39**, **E59** and **E60** are the only rows whose C behaviour is not a\nvalue the standard (or the generated code) pins down:\n\n* **E39 / E59** — `c2GJK` declares `c2Proxy pA; c2Proxy pB;` as *uninitialised*\n  automatic storage and `c2MakeProxy`'s `switch` has no `default:`. With an\n  out-of-range `C2_TYPE`, or with a stale cache index pointing past the vertices\n  the proxy actually filled in, the C code reads whatever the stack happens to\n  hold. There is no value the Rust can copy. The Rust translation zero-fills the\n  proxy (`c2Proxy::default()`) and clamps the vertex index (`ix()`), which keeps\n  the FFI boundary memory-safe.\n  The tests therefore assert what *is* well defined and observable for these\n  rows: (a) `c2MakeProxy` called directly with an out-of-range type leaves the\n  caller's buffer byte-for-byte unmodified in **both** libraries (E01), (b)\n  `c2Collided` with out-of-range types returns `0` from both without touching\n  the shape pointers (E02–E05), and (c) `c2GJK` with an out-of-range type and a\n  stale/oversized cache index does not crash either library. The\n  value-comparison is deliberately skipped for the uninitialised-read part, and\n  that is recorded here rather than silently ignored.\n* **E60** — `cache->count > 3` makes the C read `iA[3]` (one past the array,\n  landing on `iB[0]`) and write `saveA[3]` (one past `saveA`). `c2GJK` itself\n  never produces such a cache (it writes `cache->count = s.count` with\n  `s.count <= 3`), so this is unreachable through the public API. The Rust\n  clamps the loop to 3. The test asserts only that neither library crashes.\n\nEvery other row (E01–E38, E40–E58) is fully defined and is asserted\n**bit-for-bit** between the two libraries